A reentrant lock does allow a thread that already holds the lock to acquire it again (i.e., call lock.lock() multiple times). 
This is precisely what "reentrant" means.
However, the "point of the lock" still holds true because:
 * Mutual Exclusion for Other Threads: While the thread that holds the lock can re-enter, no other thread can acquire the lock 
 until the original thread has released it the same number of times it acquired it. This is the core purpose of any lock: 
 to ensure that only one thread (or in the case of reentrant, one thread at a time) can execute a critical section of code, 
 thereby preventing race conditions and data corruption.
 * Handling Recursive Calls: Reentrant locks are incredibly useful when you have methods that might directly or indirectly call 
 other methods that also need to acquire the same lock. Without reentrancy, if a thread already held a lock and then tried to 
 acquire it again (perhaps in a nested method call), it would deadlock itself. A reentrant lock allows this recursive acquisition to 
 happen seamlessly.
   Consider this example:
   class MyResource {
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private int counter = 0;

    public void incrementAndLog() {
        lock.lock(); // First acquisition
        try {
            counter++;
            logCurrentState(); // This method also needs the lock
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    private void logCurrentState() {
        lock.lock(); // Second acquisition by the same thread
        try {
            System.out.println("Current counter: " + counter);
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}

 In this example, if incrementAndLog() is called, it acquires the lock. Then, inside incrementAndLog(), logCurrentState() is called,
 which also tries to acquire the same lock. If the lock wasn't reentrant, the thread would block itself indefinitely trying to acquire
 a lock it already holds. With a reentrant lock, it successfully acquires it a second time.
In summary:
The point of a reentrant lock is still to enforce mutual exclusion and protect shared resources. Its reentrancy feature is a convenience 
and necessity for specific programming patterns (like recursive calls or methods calling other synchronized methods) that would otherwise
 lead to deadlocks if the lock were not reentrant. It doesn't negate the fundamental purpose of the lock in preventing concurrent access
  from different threads.